WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.600
Is that a hand grenade or an Easter egg?

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.340
In "Halo," the answer is "both."

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:08.870
"Halo" is bringing the video game series to
life in ways large and small on Paramount+,

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.330
and only real fans will recognize the series'
deeper references.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:15.480
These are the Easter eggs you missed in "Halo"
episode 1.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:19.949
"Halo" starts out on the planet Madrigal -- one
of humanity's outer colonies and a major center

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:22.140
for the rebellion against the UNSC.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.630
In the opening scenes of the show, Madrigal
is shown to be relatively barren, save for

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.500
occasional mining colonies.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.520
This peaceful façade is shattered by a surprise
attack by the Covenant, who kill all but one

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.840
inhabitant of the settlement they target.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.379
The Sangheili warriors (more commonly known
as "elites" in the games) are in turn dispatched

00:00:38.379 --> 00:00:42.360
by the UNSC Spartan squad Silver Team, led
by the Master Chief.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.550
"Spartans."

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:47.350
Madrigal may not ring any bells for newcomers
to the "Halo" franchise, or even for those

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:51.040
who've played the games, but the planet does
play a significant role in the spinoff novels

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.660
-- particularly Joseph Staten's "Halo: Contact
Harvest" and Tobias Buckell's "Halo: The Cole

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:56.660
Protocol."

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:00.220
In the original canon, Madrigal is one of
the first planets to be "glassed" by the covenant

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:04.470
-- a genocidal act of orbital attack that
eliminates all life on a world and renders

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:05.860
it essentially uninhabitable.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:10.670
The show's version of Madrigal is a bit different,
as it remains un-glassed well into humanity's

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:11.700
war with the Covenant.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.610
However, with a heavy narrative focus still
being placed on the planet, its original fate

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:19.740
may eventually catch up with it on Paramount+
as well.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.310
The first episode of "Halo" kicks into high
gear almost immediately with a fierce battle

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.570
between Silver Team and the Covenant's Sangheili
elites.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:29.509
It's a great way to show off the kind of action
fans can expect from the series early on,

00:01:29.509 --> 00:01:33.090
and it also features a number of specific
references to the "Halo" video games.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.740
Several of those Easter eggs come in the form
of the Covenant's weapons and sci-fi gear,

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.530
much of which will be instantly recognizable
for longtime fans.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:43.880
While the design of the Sangheili armor is
a bit different in the show (one of very few

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:48.920
design divergences), their weapons and shields
are spot-on, right down to the little details.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.390
Anyone who's played the "Halo" games on the
higher difficulties of "heroic" or "legendary"

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:55.950
will empathize with the Madrigal militia's
inability to penetrate the elites' shields

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:00.860
-- defenses that are heavily resistant to
bullets but more susceptible to plasma weapons.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:05.049
The new series does a great job of keeping
these qualities of the shields consistent.

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:08.500
Another great touch in the Madrigal fight
is the plasma pistol -- a classic Covenant

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.050
weapon from the games.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:13.480
When one of the Silver Team Spartans fires
a charged shot from the plasma pistol at an

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:17.550
enemy Sangheili, a vent on the top of the
gun pops open to vent the excess heat, just

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.650
as it does in the games.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.940
Additionally, the Madrigal fight features
incredibly faithful recreations of the energy

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.720
sword and the elites' active camouflage.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.550
If you thought the "Halo" show would break
the trend of awkward first-person sequences

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.080
in video game adaptations, you'd be wrong.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.770
The first episode barely gets 10 minutes deep
before the camera jumps inside Master Chief's

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:40.130
helmet to show a heads-up display, or HUD,
that's very reminiscent of the video games,

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:43.860
right down to a radar in the bottom left-hand
corner, targeting reticles, and a readout

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.860
of his currently equipped weapon and available
ammunition.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.500
It's a bit silly, but that doesn't stop the
show from using the HUD view repeatedly through

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:51.640
the Madrigal battle.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.960
Although the first-person view itself feels
a bit heavy-handed in its callback to the

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.090
games, there's a more subtle Easter egg incorporated
into the scene that only fans of the games

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:00.090
will recognize.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:04.420
At one point, Master Chief takes a heavy amount
of plasma fire from an attacking Elite, and

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:07.570
his HUD shows that his energy shields are
temporarily depleted.

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:11.510
He quickly ducks behind the nearest available
cover to wait for his shields to come back.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:15.040
This isn't just a nod to the mechanics of
the "Halo" games -- which were some of the

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.470
first to introduce the concept of regenerating
shields instead of a traditional health pack

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:23.090
system -- it also uses the exact same sound
effects for the shields being broken and subsequently

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:24.270
coming back.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:28.350
"Kai, hold your position."

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:33.920
The "Halo" show uses a number of specific
audio cues from the games, and the shield

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.400
noise is sure to flash players back to all
the times they had to hide behind cover.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.012
Within just the first action sequence of the
"Halo" TV series, Master Chief and his Spartan

00:03:42.012 --> 00:03:46.080
comrades exhibit a wide array of moves and
tactics pulled directly from the video games.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.750
Many viewers will recognize the weapons themselves,
which include the standard-issue UNSC assault

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:54.220
rifle, sniper rifle, handgun, and designated
marksman rifle.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:57.260
But the allusions to the games go further
than simply the guns themselves.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:01.540
The way Master Chief fights on Madrigal is
spot-on to the combat flow "Halo" players

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:02.540
know so well.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:06.260
Because of his armor and shields, Master Chief
maneuvers pretty freely in the open, only

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:08.720
using cover when his defenses are briefly
knocked out.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.250
He also makes good use of hand grenades and
melee attacks -- two key pieces of the so-called

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:16.680
"combat puzzle" that's always made the gameplay
in "Halo" so engaging.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:20.600
One specific sequence has Master Chief toss
a grenade at a group of elites, use it as

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:24.700
cover to move in close, and deliver some brutal
physical blows with the butt of his rifle

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.440
that batter down the enemy's shields.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:30.540
It's an approach anyone who's played the games
will be familiar with, even if it might not

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.060
be so easy to pull off at home.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:36.200
To top it off, Master Chief picks up a fallen
minigun dislodged from its mount and wields

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:41.430
it like it's nothing -- a specific reference
to a mechanic implemented in "Halo 3."

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:44.910
In their investigation of the Covenant ship
on Madrigal, Master Chief and Silver Team

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:48.430
happen upon an excavation site and a mysterious
artifact at its center.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:52.310
The device turns out to be a "keystone" -- a
relic from the ancient Forerunner race with

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:55.030
ties to the mythical and deadly Halo rings.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:58.780
All of this lore, only alluded to in the show's
first episode, harkens back to the games in

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:03.560
interesting ways, but there's a specific Easter
egg mixed into the scene that only eagle-eyed

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.030
viewers will catch.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:09.250
When Master Chief first activates the keystone
by touching it, a symbol appears: A relatively

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.500
nondescript circle within a circle.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:15.310
This symbol is actually the logo for the video
game "Marathon," another property developed

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:17.930
by Bungie in the years before the release
of "Halo."

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:21.650
Curiously, "Marathon" features many narrative
similarities with "Halo" and can be seen in

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.540
some ways as an elaborate prototype for the
franchise.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:29.510
Themes like human colonization of space, alien
wars, and rogue AI all play into the "Marathon"

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:31.470
story, just as they do in "Halo."

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:35.531
One of the more interesting parts of the "Halo"
show is that it features scenes from the Covenant

00:05:35.531 --> 00:05:38.060
side of the war as early as the first episode.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:42.460
These scenes take place on High Charity, the
massive spaceship/planetoid that serves as

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:44.380
the capital of the alien force.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:48.540
High Charity appears prominently in the "Halo"
games, as do the Prophets -- the enigmatic

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:52.120
race of older, wizened spacefarers who lead
the Covenant forces.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:55.639
The show's version of the vessel is pretty
much an exact match to the High Charity of

00:05:55.639 --> 00:05:59.389
the games -- a bulbous, mushroom-like structure
of the Covenant's signature purple.

00:05:59.389 --> 00:06:03.620
The "Halo" scenes aboard High Charity in the
series' early episodes include a number of

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:07.659
smaller Easter eggs for game fans as well,
such as references to the "Great Journey"

00:06:07.659 --> 00:06:11.710
-- the core belief of the Covenant religion,
which is tied to the Halo rings -- and specific

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:14.570
characters like the Prophet of Mercy and the
Prophet of Truth.

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:18.810
A major change in the Paramount+ "Halo" series
is the human character Makee, who appears

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:20.920
to be a genuine ally of the Covenant.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:24.380
Her inclusion is one of the major departures
from the games, in which the Covenant was

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:26.680
never known to accept a human into their ranks.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:30.110
It remains to be seen why this particular
human was.

